The 2025-11-20 account gets only the 2026-04-01 change undone: a plain `amount` plus `currency`, but the new status name. The oldest account gets both changes undone. Every account sees `risk_score`: adding a field needed no entry, because clients ignore names they don't know. The core code only ever builds the newest shape. The price is a list of change functions that grows with every breaking change, and is rarely cleaned out.

sections.body:
gRPC, which Google gave away as free software in 2015, is RPC again, with the network kept in view. You describe the service in a `.proto` file. A generator turns the file into a client stub and a server base class, which you extend with the real work, for each programming language:

```text
package payments.v1;

service Charges {
  rpc Charge(ChargeRequest) returns (ChargeReply);
  rpc WatchBatch(BatchRef) returns (stream BatchProgress);
}

message ChargeRequest {
  string payment_id = 1;
  int64 amount_cents = 2;
  string idempotency_key = 3;
}
```

The file says that the service `Charges` has two methods, and what a `ChargeRequest` holds. The messages are Protobuf, a compact binary format that sends each field's number instead of its name (Schemas that evolve). So they change by that lesson's rules: new fields get new numbers, and old readers skip numbers they don't know.

Each call travels over HTTP/2, the second version of HTTP, which carries many requests on one connection by giving each its own stream, a numbered lane. A call is a POST to the path `/payments.v1.Charges/Charge`. Each message goes as one flag byte (it says whether the message is compressed), a four-byte length and the Protobuf bytes. The outcome comes last, as `grpc-status` in the trailers, headers sent after the last message. A stream can carry many messages each way. So a method can be unary, one request and one reply like `Charge`, or streaming, like `WatchBatch`, which gets a reply each time the night's batch of payments to the shops moves on.

For payments, the part that matters most is the **deadline**: the moment after which the client stops waiting. gRPC sets none unless you do. So a call whose reply is lost can wait forever, and hold a thread, one path of work in the program, the whole time. The client sets one per call, for example `withDeadlineAfter(300, MILLISECONDS)`.

The deadline travels in a header, `grpc-timeout`, as time remaining, not as a time on the clock, so the two machines' clocks don't have to agree. It's like saying on the phone, "I can hold for five minutes." While the server works on the request, it may call other services. grpc-java (gRPC's library for Java and Kotlin) and its library for the Go language pass the time that's left on to those calls. A shop does the same when it tells its own supplier, "I need it within three minutes." When the time runs out, the client gets `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED`, and the server's side of the call is cancelled. The Try below sends one deadline through three programs.

Every call ends with one of 17 status codes, `OK` among them. Four others decide whether to try again. `UNAVAILABLE` usually means the request or the reply didn't get through, and the gRPC docs call it most likely temporary. `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED` means nobody knows what happened. `INVALID_ARGUMENT` (the request itself is wrong) and `FAILED_PRECONDITION` (the server's state doesn't allow it) come back the same however often you ask. gRPC can also retry by itself, following a retry policy, a set of rules you give it; the worked example near the end sets one up.

So a gRPC client opens one connection, keeps it for a long time and sends every call over it. It also fools the box that's meant to spread the calls out. Next, we'll watch it happen.

